1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,221
Book Four, Proposition Six.

2
00:00:02,221 --> 00:00:04,821
In a given circle to inscribe a square.

3
00:00:04,821 --> 00:00:10,890
Let A B C D be the given circle; it is required to inscribe a square in the circle A B C D.

4
00:00:11,310 --> 00:00:20,096
Draw two diameters A C and B D of the circle A B C D at right angles to one another through the center E.

5
00:00:20,096 --> 00:00:23,360
Join the chords A B, B C, C D, and D A.

6
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:35,145
Because B E equals E D, since E is the center, and E A is common and at right angles, by Side-Angle-Side congruence the base A B equals the base A D.

7
00:00:35,145 --> 00:00:43,382
For the same reason, B C and C D are also equal to A B and A D, so the quadrilateral A B C D is equilateral.

8
00:00:43,382 --> 00:00:57,264
Next, because B D is a diameter, B A D is a semicircle; therefore by Three Thirty-One the angle B A D is right, and for the same reason angles A B C, B C D, and C D A are also right.

9
00:00:57,264 --> 00:01:08,018
Being both equilateral and right-angled, A B C D is a square by Definition Twenty-Two, and it has been inscribed in the given circle A B C D.

10
00:01:08,018 --> 00:01:09,620
Which was to be done.

11
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:14,677
With Proposition Four Six, Euclid shifts from general triangles to regular polygons.

12
00:01:14,677 --> 00:01:25,937
As Heath explains, while any triangle can be inscribed in a circle up to similarity, an equiangular quadrilateral inscribed in a circle is indeterminate in form unless the given quadrilateral is a square.

13
00:01:25,937 --> 00:01:40,840
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred fifty-eight pairs Side-Angle-Side congruence for equal sides with Thales' semicircle theorem for right angles, becoming the foundational exhaustion step for cones and cylinders in Book Twelve, Propositions Ten through Fifteen.

14
